System audio on the PureDarwin M1 guest: coreaudiod over the PDAppleSound device nodes
=====================================================================================

What is here
------------
coreaudiod-dev-backend.patch   the tree's coreaudiod with a second kind of engine: a character
                               device that takes 16-bit frames (applies to the pinned tree, -p1)
build-sysaudio.nix             coreaudiod (patched), CoreAudio/AudioToolbox/AudioUnit and pcmplay
                               (unchanged), pdtone and pdvolume, arm64
user/pdtone.c                  a tone through an AudioQueue, reports the rate the queue ran at
user/pdvolume.c                get | set <0..100> | up | down | mute | unmute | toggle-mute
tools/sa_boot.sh               one boot with all of it staged; tools/sa_summary.sh reads a console
guest/darwin-sysaudio-test.in  unattended proof; -jack.in the default output following the jack
                               (stand-ins); -listen.in the script for a person

The path of a sample
--------------------
client (AudioQueue / AudioUnit / IOProc)
  -> AudioConverter in the client: its format to interleaved float at the device's rate
  -> the client's shared ring (pd_audio_ring, 4 periods of 512 frames, float)
  -> coreaudiod, one thread per device: sums one period of every running client, applies
     the software level when the device has no hardware volume, clips, rounds to S16LE
  -> write() to /dev/pdaudio -> the driver's FIFO -> DMA ring -> CS42L83

Nothing of this needs IOAudioFamily. An engine of the new kind is made for every node of
sDevTable that exists, by a poll thread (every 200 ms, so a node published after coreaudiod
started is found), and only while no IOAudioEngine is there; the IOAudioFamily code is as it
was (three places ask "is this a device engine": applyVolume, hostTimeOfFrame, startIOThread,
and the software gain now looks at the device's flags, which say the same for the family's
engines).

Rates: the device runs at 48000 Hz (DEV_RATE; coreaudiod sets kern.pd_sound.rate when the
driver is idle and keeps whatever the driver has if it cannot). A client at 44100 Hz is
converted in the client: the ring a client gets is at the device's rate, and AudioQueue and
the output units put an AudioConverter (linear interpolation) in front of it. coreaudiod's
own converter (mixClient, also linear) runs only for a ring whose rate differs from the
device it lands on, which happens when the default output moves to a device at another rate
under a client that follows the default. Both are linear interpolation without a low-pass:
good enough for system sounds, audibly rough on dense high-frequency material; a better
resampler belongs in AudioConverter.c and mixClient, not here.

Pacing and timestamps
---------------------
The driver's FIFO takes 16384 frames (341 ms) before a write blocks. Filling it would make
every sound a third of a second late, so the server keeps DEV_QUEUE_FRAMES (3072, 64 ms)
queued instead and follows the driver's own clock: kern.pd_sound.frames counts the frames
the DMA has played, and the driver takes one period (1024) from the FIFO for each period
played, so
    queued = frames written - (kern.pd_sound.frames now - at the start)
The thread reads the counter (one sysctl by MIB) and writes a period of 512 whenever the
queue has room for one, else sleeps 2 ms. The device's clock is therefore the only clock:
no drift between host time and the codec, and AudioQueue callbacks run at the device's rate
because the rings empty at it.

  start: a session of the driver begins with about 1.1 s of silence while the codec's
    converter locks, during which the FIFO is not read. The server writes its 3072 frames
    and waits until the driver says the session runs and has locked (playing, sessions,
    lock_ms; preroll_ms + 1 s is the bound), then starts counting. Clients are held, their
    first frames are not lost.
  underrun: when the count shows the driver took more than was written (the server was
    late), the missed frames are not made up: the queue starts from empty again, so the
    latency does not creep. Counted and logged ("late by N frames").
  stall: a counter that does not move for 2 s while the queue is full closes the node; it
    is reopened a second later.
  idle: with no client playing nothing is written (the driver plays silence while the node
    is open); DEV_LINGER_SECONDS (5) after the last client the node is closed, so a new
    sound within that time starts at once and a later one pays the 1.1 s start.
  timestamps: readHostTime of a client's ring is now + (queued + 512 + DEV_OUTPUT_FRAMES)
    frames, DEV_OUTPUT_FRAMES (5120) being the periods the driver fills ahead of the DMA.
    The device's latency is reported as 8192 frames (171 ms).

A device without the frames sysctl is paced by the host clock (one period per period time,
write() blocking when the device's queue is full).

Volume
------
Headphones: kPDAudioDeviceHardwareVolume | HardwareMute. Level 0..1 maps linearly to the
codec's attenuation, kern.pd_sound.volume_db = round((1 - level) * 62): 0 dB at 1.0, 62 dB
at 0.0; mute writes 63 (the driver's mute) and keeps the level. At the first start the level
is read back from the driver (30 dB -> 0.516), so starting the server changes nothing that
can be heard; later starts use /Library/Preferences/org.puredarwin.audio.plist.
A device without hardware volume (speakers) is scaled in the mix, level squared, and starts
at 0.5 (DEV_SOFT_VOLUME: -12 dB) until a level is set.

Adding the speakers
-------------------
The table already has the entry:
    { "Speakers", "PDAudio:speakers", "/dev/pdspeaker", ..., "kern.pd_speaker", false, kDevJackEmpty }
so the device appears by itself once the node exists. For the same pacing as the headphones
the driver should give kern.pd_speaker.frames, .playing, .sessions, .lock_ms (or always 0),
.preroll_ms and .rate with the meaning they have under kern.pd_sound; without frames the
host clock paces and the FIFO slowly fills or empties with the clock difference (a blocked
write or a short silence every few minutes). The software level is not a protection: what
keeps the speakers safe has to stay in the kernel.

The default output follows the jack (implemented, devPollThread/devRouteLocked): every
200 ms kern.pd_sound.jack is read; when it changes, or a device appears, the default output
and the default system output become the kDevJackPlugged device while something is plugged
(jack > 0) and the kDevJackEmpty device otherwise, each only when that device exists. On
this machine today only Headphones exists, so it stays the default with an empty jack. A
default set by hand (kPDAudioSetDefaultOutput) holds until the next jack event.

What happens to running clients at a switch: a client on the default device (every
AudioQueue and default output unit: the alias device 0x40) keeps its ring and its rate; at
its next period the other device's thread mixes it. The device left is closed 5 s later;
the device taken is opened at once (for the headphones: 1.1 s of start during which the
client is held, nothing is dropped; what was already written to the device left is played
there, up to 64 ms plus the driver's periods). A client that connected to a device by its
id stays on it. Listeners get kAudioHardwarePropertyDefaultOutputDevice and the alias
device's volume and mute change notifications, as for any default change.

The switch can be tried without the hardware: COREAUDIOD_SPEAKER_DEVICE=<node> names a
stand-in for /dev/pdspeaker (/dev/null works: paced by the host clock) and
COREAUDIOD_JACK_FILE=<file> a file holding the jack's number in place of the sysctl
(guest/darwin-sysaudio-jack.in).

Adding capture
--------------
Driver: a read-only node, say /dev/pdmic: S16LE at kern.pd_mic.rate, channels fixed and
told by a sysctl (kern.pd_mic.channels), read() blocking until frames are there, capture
running only while the node is open; kern.pd_mic.frames (captured) and .overruns. The
headset microphone (kern.pd_sound.jack == 2) would be a second node.

coreaudiod (small): a direction in dev_desc; an engine with kPDAudioDeviceHasInput and
inputChannels; a thread that reads periods, converts to float and writes them into the ring
of every client connected for input, signalling its semaphore. The ring is the one that
exists with the roles turned round (the server moves writeFrame, the client readFrame; a
client that does not read loses the oldest frames). The connect message has a spare word
(pad) for the direction; kPDAudioFollowDefaultInput next to kPDAudioFollowDefaultOutput.
Default input, kPDAudioSetDefaultInput and inputChannels are already in the protocol. The
node is opened while an input client runs, as for output.

Frameworks (the larger part; today there is no input anywhere):
  CoreAudio   AudioHardware.c answers input-scope properties with "no property"
              (streams, stream configuration, stream format, latency): they need the
              input side of the device info. AudioDeviceIO.c calls IOProcs with a NULL
              input list: it needs an input session per device and to hand each cycle's
              captured frames to the procs.
  AudioToolbox AudioQueueNewInput returns kAudioQueueErr_InvalidDevice. It needs the
              mirror of the output queue: an IOProc on the default input device, an
              AudioConverter from the device's float to the queue's format, filling the
              enqueued buffers and calling the input callback with the packet count.
              The output unit (AUHAL) needs kAudioOutputUnitProperty_EnableIO on element 1
              and an input callback with AudioUnitRender from the captured frames.

Known limits
------------
  - One writer: /dev/pdaudio opens once. While coreaudiod holds it pdplay gets EBUSY, and
    while something else holds it coreaudiod retries once a second.
  - kern.pd_sound.tone and a second user of the driver's rate are outside the server's
    knowledge: the driver's test tone is not heard while the server writes (the driver
    plays its FIFO first), and a rate changed while the node is closed is set back at the
    next open.
  - The output is clipped, not limited, when clients sum above full scale.
